# Windows shells seek a batch file by byte offset and count those offsets
# assuming CRLF. Given LF-only endings, cmd.exe resumes each command a little
# further into the file than it should and ends up executing the tail of a line:
# users reported 'rts' is not recognized, which is the end of "…first run, starts
# the web server…" — a rem comment, escaping its own rem.
#
# Checked out with CRLF everywhere, not only on Windows, so that editing the file
# on Linux cannot quietly put it back the way it was.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The other side of the same coin: a shell script with CRLF fails on the shebang
# line with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M".
*.sh text eol=lf

# A ZIP from GitHub carries no .git, so the program cannot ask git which build it
# is — and a footer that names no commit leaves a reader unable to say what they
# are running. "Download ZIP" is `git archive`, which substitutes $Format:…$
# placeholders, so the answer travels inside the archive itself.
build-info.json export-subst
